WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:01.734
-Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:02.501
We're joined right now by one

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.734
of the most renowned chefs

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:04.968
in the world.

00:00:04.968 --> 00:00:05.801
[ Drumroll ]
He's an Emmy

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:07.667
and James Beard Award winner.

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:09.834
His latest book,
"Art of the Chicken,"

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:11.567
will be released
later this fall.

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.467
Everyone, please welcome
Chef Jacques Pépin!

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:18.133
[ Cheering and applause ]
[ Rimshot ]

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.434
-Oh, stop.
I'm so excited.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:23.334
We're so honored and happy
that you're here.

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:25.467
Thank you so much
for coming on the show

00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:27.667
and I'm here with Questlove
right here

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.367
and I want to ask you
a thousand questions.

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:31.934
I mean, you've cooked
for world leaders,

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:34.634
everything, restaurants.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:38.467
Julia Child said you're
the greatest chef in America.

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.200
-That's a lot.
-Now, what is this experience?

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.501
Exactly. Where does
"The Tonight Show" sit with you?

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:44.167
[ Laughter ]
-This is actually not
a cookbook.

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:45.701
It's a book of drawing.

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:50.601
I have 130 drawing of chicken
and story about chicken and egg.

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:52.200
-You did this right here?

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.234
You drew this?

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:54.467
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:55.767
I do a lot of drawing.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:56.868
Planning and so forth.

00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:58.734
-God, that's beautiful.
-Thank you.

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:01.133
-You've been teaching people
for years

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.968
all sorts of how to cook
and everything.

00:01:03.968 --> 00:01:07.934
And then, can you tell us
what the Pépin Foundation does?

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.667
The Jacques Pépin Foundation.
-Oh.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.834
We try to work with coming
into the kitchen

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:13.901
with people who have been
kind of

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:16.000
disenfranchised by life.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.167
People coming out of jail,
former drug addicts,

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:22.234
homeless people,
even veterans, and so forth.

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:26.434
So it's not young people --
30, 40, 50 years old.

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:28.701
My son-in-law and my daughter
are doing it.

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:29.834
They are the greatest.

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:32.267
I would never have done it
without them.

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:33.634
-Wow!
It's amazing what you do

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:34.400
just go give back.
-Thank you.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.400
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:39.901
-Now, right now,

00:01:39.901 --> 00:01:41.267
I was just happy
that you're here.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.601
I was actually just even going
to sit and interview you

00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:46.300
and I said, I said,
"Well, let's cook something."

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.334
Can you teach us?
What are you going to teach us

00:01:48.334 --> 00:01:50.534
how to do,
or or show us how to do?

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:51.501
-I think an omelet.

00:01:51.501 --> 00:01:52.734
I'm going to show you how
to make

00:01:52.734 --> 00:01:54.267
a classic French omelet.

00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:56.601
-Classic French omelet.
-Yes, it's a bit different.

00:01:56.601 --> 00:01:59.634
The first thing we do,
we put that on.

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:03.467
So you you just break it down
and its on.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:05.234
And put a piece of butter
in there.

00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:07.300
-Oh, you know what?
-We get the color of those eggs.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.934
-You showed me how to crack
an egg, by the way,

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:10.634
the right way.
-Oh.

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:12.567
You said don't break it
on the edge.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:14.267
Don't crack it
on the edge of a bowl.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.167
-Exactly.
-Crack it on the flat

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.634
because less shell?

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.167
-Yeah, because you put
the shell inside,

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:23.334
you introduce bacteria very
often, or you break the yolk.

00:02:23.334 --> 00:02:25.167
-Yes, you put three in there.

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:26.167
Check this out right here.
Look at this.

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:27.834
Watch, watch, ready?
Watch this.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.334
[ Laughter and cheering ]
-Wow! He does it with one hand.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:33.167
-Okay.

00:02:34.501 --> 00:02:37.133
-So we want to beat it
and you want to beat it enough

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:39.534
so you don't have long string
of egg whites, you know?

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:40.601
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:41.567
-Beat it so you don't have

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:42.667
long strings of egg whites.

00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:43.734
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:46.634
-Okay, so, now,
my butter is melted.

00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:47.934
-Oh, I didn't even
put butter in there.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:50.133
We were supposed to put butter
in there, of course.

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:51.667
-I got you.
-You put butter in there?

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:52.400
-I put butter in it.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.367
-And then, you move

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:54.634
with both hands.

00:02:54.634 --> 00:02:56.601
-What in the world?
-Whoa, slow down, slow down!

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:58.067
[ Laughter ]
-What's going on?
Alright. Hold on.

00:02:58.067 --> 00:02:59.834
-Okay.
-Butter's in there.

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.734
Alright, so --

00:03:01.734 --> 00:03:04.334
You're getting some volume!
Getting some volume in yours!

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.868
What the heck is going on?!

00:03:05.868 --> 00:03:07.434
Hold on, butter out.
-At the end now.

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:09.000
-Wait, I'm putting egg in.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.467
What did you just mix that with?

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.000
-Some spinach inside.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.234
[ Laughter ]
What were making

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:14.634
that noise with?
-I fold the lip.

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:15.868
[ Laughter ]
-Yes!

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:17.801
Quest, is yours looking
like this?

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:18.901
-No!
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:20.934
-Yeah, you fold one lip
on the other lip.

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:24.267
You bang it this way.
That bring it on.

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.667
-Wait, are you playing drums
right now?

00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:27.767
[ Laughter ]
-That's how you make an omelet!

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:29.434
Have you ever been to France,
Quest?!

00:03:29.434 --> 00:03:31.033
[ Laughter ]
-That's it, that's it.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:33.567
Then you fold it.
Now, you fold it.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:35.534
Oh, ow, ay, ay, ay, ay.

00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:36.701
-Alright.

00:03:36.701 --> 00:03:38.801
[ Laughter and ohs ]

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.133
Right, we have more butter
on that one.

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:40.901
Oh.
-Ope.

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.367
Look at this.
And then

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.934
cheese.

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:46.033
-Oh, I'm supposed to
-Wow!

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:47.267
-not eat the shells, okay.

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:48.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. And there you go.

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:49.267
A little cheese on top.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:50.767
-Sorry about that.
-Or in the middle.

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:52.701
Look at this.
It kind of looks similar.

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:53.901
Ran out of forks.

00:03:53.901 --> 00:03:56.267
-You call that an omelet?

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:57.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:01.133
-Which one is by the chef
and which one was done by me?

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:02.234
I mean --

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:03.701
[ Laughter ]
-Oh.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.934
[ Applause ]
-We made scrambled eggs.

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:07.834
-He chose mine, though!

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:09.267
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:10.934
-I love scrambled eggs this way.

00:04:10.934 --> 00:04:12.701
[ Rimshot ]
They're even better this way.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:14.701
-He loves scrambled eggs
that way.

00:04:14.701 --> 00:04:17.501
-That's right!
-The one and only Chef --

00:04:17.501 --> 00:04:18.434
You want to try mine?

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:21.000
-Yeah.
-I have to try yours.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.067
-Oh.

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:23.234
-Don't answer.

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:24.300
You didn't say anything.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.267
Let me try yours.

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:28.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.868
-I think yours tastes
better than mine.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.067
-Nuh-unh.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:32.267
That's the most delicious thing

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:33.667
[ Cheering and applause ]
I've ever had in my life.

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:35.267
-Thank you.

00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:36.667
-Jacques Pépin!

00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:40.300
Ooh!
-Shall we have some wine?

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.801
-Oh, now you can come back

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.701
whenever you like.
I love you.

00:04:43.701 --> 00:04:45.067
Jacques Pépin.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.501
His book "Art of the Chicken"

00:04:46.501 --> 00:04:48.834
will be available
September 13th.

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:51.067
To learn more about the
Jacques Pépin Foundation,

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.133
go to jp.foundation.

